{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/938733-virtue-and-vice-as-conditions","id":938733,"title":"Virtue and Vice as Conditions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-07-28T04:40:57-07:00","id":938733,"title":"Virtue and Vice as Conditions","content":"I have been thinking of homebrewing Virtues and Vices for my games, inspired by the use of character Aspects in Fate Core as well as the excellent thread in these forums of fan-made Conditions. We can easily adapt any Virtue or Vice into a Condition and have them fulfill the exact same functions. I will write a few examples below:<br /> <br /> Generous<br /> Your character is deeply generous and has not only the will but indeed a need to share his fortunes and time with others. Gain a Willpower Point when you are able to substantially improve the situation of another with your generousity.<br /> Resolution: Your generousity backlashes severely. Money donated to charity actually ended up benefitting local crime gangs, someone bluffed about being in great need to take advantage of you, or your generousity has left you severely weakened in face of a great obstacle or danger which causes you great regret. If this backlash is severe enough, your character may take a beat and choose a new Condition such as &quot;Bitter&quot;, &quot;Bereft of Hope&quot; or similar.<br /> <br /> Greedy<br /> Your character is possessed with having more and cannot be satisfied. This may be money, power, status, fame, knowledge or something else. Gain a Willpower Point when your character succeeds in gaining something important to her that she craves a lot.<br /> Resolution: Your greed gets the better of you; you reach too far, lose too much or put yourself in great danger because of it. If this is enough to ultimately make your character change her ways and rethink her greed, resolve this Condition, take a beat and take a new Condition that reflects her newfound self.<br /> <br /> Alternately, one or more Virtue / Vice may be folded into one single &quot;Archetype Condition&quot;. The example below is a Condition incorporating the Virtue of Generousity and the Vice of Greed.<br /> <br /> Robin Hood<br /> Your character wishes to redistribute wealth, plain and simple. She may be an ardent defender and fighter for social justice, a vigilante or an entrepreneur with thoughts of growing her company while at the same time do good for society or the people around her. Gain a Willpower Point when your character manages to make a huge profit by whatever means necessary (theft, trade, robbery, lawsuit etc.), if this is not done at the expense of those who are worse off. Gain a WIllpower Point when she can improve the lives of those who are worse off than her, especially with wealth taken from those who do not deserve it (from her point of view).<br /> Resolution: (basically the same thing as described with Generous and Greedy, but put together)<br /> <br /> Main goal of this homebrew:<br /> My goal with this homebrew is more or less about aesthetics. I want to eliminate unnecesary complexity by folding the Virtue/Vice system into the excellent Conditions system, making the game more elegant as a whole.<br /> Second, I never liked the &quot;Christian morality&quot; that is embedded in Virtues / Vices even though the 2nd edition moved away from the overtly biblical 7 virtues and 7 vices. I cannot quite shake the feeling that the game itself tells us that Generousity is &quot;good&quot; and Greed is &quot;evil&quot; by naming them Virtues and Vices.<br /> Third, I have never been a fan of morality systems in RPGs, and have little patience for things like the Alignment System in D&amp;D. I can live without this out-of-game morality; what is good or evil is up to each character in the game.<br /> <br /> Main implications of this homebrew (that I can think of right now):<br /> Players and Storytellers need to determine how many of these &quot;Values Conditions&quot; a character may have in effect, probably taking the existing limitations of Virtues and Vices as a guideline. If an Archetype Condition like Robin Hood is used, this issue is more complicated.<br /> Players and Storytellers need to work out or improvise new effects of the various Conditions dealing with Soullessness.<br /> Instead of discovering and exploiting another characters Virtue / Vice, a PC instead discovers and exploits another character\u00b4s Conditions. It is basically the same thing when it comes to social maneuvering and the like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":9849,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9849-aristarkos","user":"Aristarkos","date":". 07-28-2016, 07:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9849-aristarkos","name":"Aristarkos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9849_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"369"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-28T11:53:47-07:00","id":938991,"title":"","content":"Well, for one thing, Virtue and Vice integrate with more things (such as effects on Social Maneuvering) than a normal Condition allows for.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}